# T004Q63: full-target attempts and exact limitations

The unchanged target is scope.md. It remains unresolved. The lower-degree guard obstructions below are not impossibility theorems for arbitrary reductions, and the degree12 construction is not a substitute nominee.

## 1. Exploration: smoothness can be obtained at degree12

Take the actual source circuit normal form Q(x)=sum_i q_i(x)^2, deg q_i≤2. It has rational zero exactly when the original input does. The nonconstant case suffices; constants can be decided and replaced by fixed smooth geometrically integral yes/no conics (x²+y²=1 and x²+y²=−1).

Write H(s)=(1+s)^4−s^4=4s³+6s²+4s+1. Its three roots −1/2, (−1+i)/2, (−1−i)/2 are nonzero and distinct. The complex critical values of Q form a finite set: on each irreducible component of the reduced critical locus dQ vanishes, so Q is constant in its characteristic-zero function field; there are finitely many components. Choose a positive rational a such that none of the roots of H divided by a is a critical value. All but finitely many positive rational choices work. It is effective to find one: enumerate a=1,2,... and test that the ideal generated by F and all its first partial derivatives is the unit ideal over Q, using Gröbner bases, for

    F(x,z)=z²−H(aQ(x)).

The complex Jacobian criterion says a singular point would have z=0, H(aQ)=0 and dQ=0, excluded by the choice. Thus this is globally smooth. H(aQ) is a nonconstant squarefree polynomial: each of its distinct level factors Q−c has no critical point on its zero set, so is squarefree, and the factors are pairwise coprime. It is therefore not a square in C(x). The monic quadratic F is irreducible over C(x) and, by Gauss's lemma, geometrically integral. Clearing rational coefficient denominators does not change the hypersurface. Its total degree is at most12.

For a rational point put s=aQ(x)≥0, t=s/(1+s), and y=z/(1+s)². Then y²=1−t⁴ and 0≤t<1. This curve has no rational point with 0<t<1. Indeed the positive rational numbers 1−t⁴, 2t², 1+t⁴ form a right triangle of square area t²(1−t⁴)=(ty)². Fermat's classical descent rules this out. For completeness: scale to an integer right triangle of square area and divide to a primitive one. Its legs are 2mn,m²−n² with coprime m>n of opposite parity. The four pairwise coprime positive integers m,n,m+n,m−n have square product, hence are u²,v²,w²,z². The last two are odd. Put A=(w+z)/2 and B=(w−z)/2. Then A²+B²=u² and 2AB=v²; v is even, so AB/2=(v/2)². This is a new integer right triangle of square area with strictly smaller hypotenuse u than m²+n², contradicting minimality. Thus t=0, Q(x)=0. Conversely Q(x)=0 gives z=±1.

This construction supplies a many-one reduction from H10(Q) to smooth geometrically integral affine degree≤12 hypersurface solvability. It does not upgrade the source's Turing reduction from H10(Z) to a many-one completeness assertion. The arithmetic curve is elementary classical input; finite critical-value avoidance and geometric-integrality arguments follow the same operation as Poonen Lemma4.1. This is assessed TECHNICAL and is not nominated.

## 2. First full-target route: circuit compression back to degree4

One can apply the source arithmetic-circuit construction to F above and obtain quadrics r_1,...,r_s defining an affine variety isomorphic to F=0. This is a genuinely smooth geometrically integral intersection of quadrics: the graph equations solve for each circuit node successively, and the final output equation recovers F. Its Jacobian has full expected rank because the graph part has the triangular identity block and the last differential is dF.

The obvious attempt to recover a single quartic is R=sum_j r_j². Over Q its zeros are exactly those of the smooth intersection. But at every such point dR=2 sum_j r_j dr_j=0. Every rational point is a singular point of the hypersurface R=0. Consequently the construction does not produce promised inputs whenever the original input has a rational zero. Deleting the singular locus loses every yes witness; taking a generic perturbation no longer preserves rational solvability. Resolving the hypersurface changes both the presentation and the degree restriction. Smoothness of the intersection cannot be confused with smoothness of the one-equation encoding.

## 3. Second full-target route: general quadratic residual guards

The preceding failure is not confined to positive squares. Suppose s≥2 and G(u_1,...,u_s,y_1,...,y_m) is any nonzero rational polynomial of total degree at most2 with the universal exact-recovery property

    G(u,y)=0 over Q implies u_1=...=u_s=0.

Then G has no nonsingular rational zero. To prove this, suppose P is one. An irreducible component Z through P of the affine quadric is defined over Q, has dimension s+m−1, and is rational with Z(Q) Zariski dense. For an irreducible quadric this follows by projection from P: on a line P+tv the second root is t=−L(v)/Q_2(v), rational in the direction v on an open set; if the quadratic part vanishes identically the component is an affine hyperplane. Reducible quadratic cases are unions of rational hyperplanes through any rational nonsingular point, and the same conclusion holds. Exact recovery would force this dense set into the closed subspace u=0 of dimension m. Since s+m−1>m this is impossible.

Thus no such universal guard is smooth at a rational zero. Substitution u_i=q_i(x), with the source quadrics q_i, gives a polynomial of degree at most4. At a rational solution its derivative is zero by the chain rule, since every partial derivative of G vanishes at the corresponding rational guard point. This excludes all universally exact quadratic guards in the residuals, with arbitrary auxiliary variables and arbitrary rational coefficients, not merely the diagonal square-sum.

The word universal matters: the guard is required to force u=0 for arbitrary rational u before substitution. An input-dependent construction exploiting special algebraic relations among the q_i may escape this obstruction. Nor does the lemma exclude a reduction preserving only existence without projecting every witness back to a zero. Neither escape has been constructed here.

## 4. Aggregate-linear guards also fail

Another natural degree4 attempt uses only the aggregate Q and auxiliary variables, F=cQ+B(y), where c≠0 and deg B≤4. If Q has exact degree4, this is the most general polynomial in the independent symbols Q,y of weighted degree≤4 with Q weight4. At a source zero, dQ=0. Smoothness of F at a rational lift y0 therefore requires B(y0)=0 and dB(y0)≠0.

No such smooth lift can enforce pointwise Q=0 universally. Take the explicit source-admissible aggregate Q=x_1²+x_2²+x_3²+x_4²+w⁴. It has degree4, a zero at the origin, and represents every nonnegative rational value, by the classical four-square theorem after clearing denominators and setting w=0. Near any rational y0 with dB(y0)≠0 there are rational y with −B(y)/c>0: move along a rational coordinate direction with nonzero derivative to the appropriate side. That value is represented by Q, producing a rational F=0 point with Q>0. This is a pointwise recovery obstruction, not a contradiction to existence equivalence for this particular yes input. A fixed guard meant to work for all aggregates can also be defeated by adding a sufficiently small positive rational constant to Q, giving a no-zero aggregate while retaining a represented value −B/c. Input-dependent choices are not ruled out by this last argument.

## 5. Remaining obligation

A proof of T004Q63 needs an effective way to preserve rational nonemptiness in a globally smooth geometrically integral single affine equation of degree≤4. It must avoid universal quadratic aggregation of residual equations, or exploit an input-dependent arithmetic mechanism that the above lemmas do not address. The degree12 curve method does not do this; applying the source's quadratic circuit normalization after it returns exactly to the singular-aggregation obstruction. No full reduction or alternative proof of undecidability on the promised class is presently available. No theorem asserting the target false has been established.

